+# Cross-agent Execution Mode compatibility test
+
+Verifies the roadmap item tracked in `.l00prite/todos.md`: *"Cross-agent compatibility
+tests, including a mid-execution boundary stop and resume by a different vendor."*
+
+The claim under test: **Agent A** (one vendor) runs an Execution Mode loop, hits a run
+boundary mid-execution, stops, and persists its state to `.l00prite/heartbeat.json`,
+`.l00prite/state.json`, and related memory files. **Agent B** (a different vendor), reading
+*only* the files in `.l00prite/` — no shared session, no shared process memory, no
+vendor-specific state — resumes from exactly where Agent A stopped: it does not redo Agent
+A's completed work, and it does not walk through (or silently ignore) the boundary Agent A
+hit.
+
+## What this deliverable actually contains, and what each part proves
+
+There are two artifacts, and they prove two different things:
+
+1. **`scripts/cross-agent-compat-test.js`** (automated, executed as part of writing this
+   document — see "Evidence from an actual run" below). It runs the exact same fixture
+   project through two independent OS processes standing in for two vendors ("Agent A" /
+   "Agent B"), using deterministic stand-in work (see "Why the fixture's tasks are
+   trivial" below) instead of real LLM judgment. **It proves the `.l00prite/` file-state
+   machine described by `templates/l00prite/prompts/execute-loop.md` is mechanically
+   resumable across a simulated vendor boundary**: locking, a fresh iteration budget on
+   re-arming, todo/ledger continuity, and honest, non-bypassable boundary persistence. It
+   also includes a `selftest` mode that deliberately runs a *non-compliant* resumption and
+   confirms the assertions actually catch it (see "Proving the test has teeth" below) —
+   without that, a script that always prints PASS would be worthless as a test.
+2. **The manual protocol** (below). This has **not** been executed in this session — this
+   run had no access to a second real, different-vendor agent CLI. It is written to be
+   precise enough that two different people (or two agent sessions) can execute it and reach
+   the same verifiable result, using two *real* different-vendor agents (e.g. Claude Code as
+   Agent A, an OpenAI Codex CLI or Gemini CLI session as Agent B) reading and following the
+   real `.l00prite/prompts/execute-loop.md` prompt, with a real human giving the required
+   pre-flight confirmation.
+
+**Honesty note:** the automated script demonstrates that the file semantics are sound and
+that a resumption which violates them (redoing work, bypassing a gate, reusing a stale
+confirmation) is mechanically detectable. It does **not** demonstrate that a real LLM agent
+will choose to comply with `execute-loop.md` when actually running it — no automated harness
+can demonstrate that without invoking a real model, which this control-arm run is
+explicitly forbidden from doing (no calls to any other model). That is exactly the gap the
+manual protocol below is for, and it is documented, not run, in this pass.
+
+## Why the fixture's tasks are trivial
+
+`tests/cross-agent-compat/fixture/.l00prite/blueprint.md` describes a "Widget CLI" with
+three requirements, each defined as "append one exact, specified line to `widget_cli.txt`"
+and "verified" by checking that the line is present. This is deliberate: it keeps the
+*testable surface* — did the resuming agent redo R1's line, did it write R2's gated line, do
+the ledger/state/heartbeat fields match — unambiguous and mechanically checkable by *both*
+the automated script and a real LLM agent following the same blueprint, without asking a
+real agent to write and grade actual code. The point of this test is the resumption
+protocol, not code generation.
+
+## Fixture layout
+
+```
+tests/cross-agent-compat/fixture/
+  README.md                    why this fixture exists, what it deliberately omits
+  .l00prite/
+    blueprint.md                R1 (widget list), R2 (widget purge --all, DESTRUCTIVE/gated), R3 (widget create)
+    todos.md                    T1, T2 (GATE: destructive-operation), T3 — all unchecked
+    heartbeat.json              schema v2, execution.enabled=false, execution.max_iterations=1
+    state.json                  schema v2, execution_active=false
+    lock.json                   status=unlocked
+    ledger.md / memory.md / constraints.md / failures.md   empty/template starting state
+    LOCKING.md                  byte-identical copy of templates/l00prite/LOCKING.md
+    prompts/execute-loop.md     byte-identical copy of templates/l00prite/prompts/execute-loop.md
+    prompts/README.md           byte-identical copy of templates/l00prite/prompts/README.md
+```
+
+`execution.max_iterations` is deliberately set to `1` so Agent A hits `iteration_limit_reached`
+after exactly one unit of work (T1), with T2 and T3 still open. T2 is tagged
+`(GATE: destructive-operation)` and named in `heartbeat.json.human_review_gates`, so whichever
+agent reaches it next must stop at `human_review_gate` rather than implement it. This gives
+the scenario **two distinct boundaries** in one story: Agent A stops at
+`iteration_limit_reached`, and Agent B — resuming — stops at a *different* boundary,
+`human_review_gate`, rather than either redoing T1 or ignoring the gate on T2.
+
+`git diff --stat` never shows changes under `tests/cross-agent-compat/fixture/` from running
+the test: every run works on a fresh `os.tmpdir()` copy and never mutates the checked-in
+fixture.
+
+## Automated harness
+
+### Preconditions
+
+- Node.js available on `PATH` (no other dependency — same constraint as
+  `scripts/validate-l00prite.js`).
+- Run from the repo root, on a clean checkout (the script reads
+  `templates/l00prite/prompts/execute-loop.md` etc. from the repo to self-check the fixture
+  hasn't drifted).
+
+### Commands
+
+```bash
+node scripts/cross-agent-compat-test.js run              # compliant resumption — expect all PASS, exit 0
+node scripts/cross-agent-compat-test.js run --violation   # non-compliant resumption — expect FAILs, exit 0
+                                                           # (exit 0 here means "the harness correctly
+                                                           #  caught the violation"; exit 1 would mean a
+                                                           #  violation slipped through undetected)
+node scripts/cross-agent-compat-test.js selftest          # runs both of the above and confirms the
+                                                           # violation run fails on exactly the checks
+                                                           # that matter; exit 0/1 is the overall verdict
+```
+
+Each `run` invocation: copies `tests/cross-agent-compat/fixture/.l00prite/` into a fresh
+`os.tmpdir()` directory, self-checks that the fixture's prompt/`LOCKING.md` copies are still
+byte-identical to canonical, snapshots the "before" state, spawns `agent-a` as a **separate
+`node` process** against that directory, snapshots and asserts the checkpoint, spawns
+`agent-b` (or `agent-b-violating`) as **another separate `node` process** — sharing nothing
+with the first except the files on disk — snapshots and asserts the final checkpoint, then
+prints a `PASS`/`FAIL` line per assertion and a final count, in the same style as
+`scripts/validate-l00prite.js`. On full success the scratch directory is deleted; on any
+failure it is left in place and its path is printed for inspection.
+
+### Pass/fail criteria (compliant run — `run`)
+
+All of the following must hold; the script's exit code is `0` iff every row below is a
+`PASS` line in its output:
+
+| # | Checkpoint | Field / file | Expected value |
+|---|------------|--------------|-----------------|
+| 1 | before Agent A | `heartbeat.json:execution.enabled` | `false` |
+| 2 | before Agent A | `state.json:execution_active` | `false` |
+| 3 | before Agent A | `widget_cli.txt` | does not exist |
+| 4 | before Agent A | `todos.md` | T1 unchecked |
+| 5 | after Agent A | `heartbeat.json:execution.enabled` | `false` (reset at mode exit) |
+| 6 | after Agent A | `heartbeat.json:execution.current_iteration` | `1` |
+| 7 | after Agent A | `heartbeat.json:execution.max_iterations` | `1` (unchanged — no self-modification) |
+| 8 | after Agent A | `heartbeat.json:execution.last_run_boundary` | `"iteration_limit_reached"` |
+| 9 | after Agent A | `state.json:execution_active` | `false` |
+| 10 | after Agent A | `state.json:execution_stop_reason` | `"iteration_limit_reached"` |
+| 11 | after Agent A | `lock.json:status` | `"released"` |
+| 12 | after Agent A | `lock.json:lock_id` | present, identifies agent-A |
+| 13 | after Agent A | `todos.md` | T1 checked; T2, T3 unchecked |
+| 14 | after Agent A | `widget_cli.txt` | exactly one T1 line, no T2 line |
+| 15 | after Agent A | `ledger.md` | contains a run entry attributed to agent-A |
+| 16 | after Agent A | `heartbeat.json:execution.preflight_confirmed_by/at` | populated for agent-A's confirmation |
+| 17 | after Agent B | `ledger.md` | Agent A's entire entry preserved byte-for-byte; file has grown |
+| 18 | after Agent B | `widget_cli.txt` | **still** exactly one T1 line (T1 not redone) |
+| 19 | after Agent B | `widget_cli.txt` | still no T2 line (gate not bypassed) |
+| 20 | after Agent B | `todos.md` | T2 still unchecked |
+| 21 | after Agent B | `heartbeat.json:execution.preflight_confirmed_by` | **different** from Agent A's — fresh confirmation, not a reused flag |
+| 22 | after Agent B | `heartbeat.json:execution.preflight_confirmed_at` | **different** (later) timestamp than Agent A's |
+| 23 | after Agent B | `lock.json:lock_id` | a newly minted id, not Agent A's reused one |
+| 24 | after Agent B | `heartbeat.json:execution.last_run_boundary` | `"human_review_gate"` |
+| 25 | after Agent B | `state.json:execution_stop_reason` | `"human_review_gate"` |
+| 26 | after Agent B | `lock.json:status` | `"released"` |
+
+(The real script asserts 33 conditions total — the table above is the load-bearing subset;
+the rest are the fixture-drift self-check and a few "before Agent A" sanity checks.)
+
+### Proving the test has teeth
+
+Rows 18–25 above are exactly the rows `run --violation` is designed to flip to `FAIL`: the
+violating Agent B reuses Agent A's stale `preflight_confirmed_at`/`by` instead of getting a
+fresh confirmation, writes to protected files without acquiring the lock, **redoes T1**
+(duplicate line in `widget_cli.txt`), and **implements the gated T2** anyway. `selftest`
+runs the compliant scenario (expect 0 failures) and the violating scenario (expect the 7
+resumption-integrity checks above to fail), and only exits `0` if both hold. This is what
+distinguishes this from a script that would print PASS regardless of what Agent B does.
+
